            <ArticleTitle>Effect of scapular protraction and retraction on isometric shoulder elevation strength.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>367-70</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine the effect of scapular protraction (SP) and scapular retraction (SR) on isometric shoulder elevation strength measured in the sagittal plane and to test the hypothesis that strength would be significantly reduced when tested in the SP position relative to the neutral resting scapular position (SN).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective before-after trial.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Multidisciplinary sports medicine center.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Ten healthy volunteers (5 men, 5 women) ages 26 to 43 years recruited from the Mayo Clinic, Rochester, MN.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Subjects completed 3 maximal isometric shoulder elevation contractions at 90 degrees of sagittal plane elevation in the SN, SP, and SR positions. The order of scapular positions was varied to minimize fatigue effects. Mean isometric strength values were compared by using Student t tests.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES" NlmCategory="METHODS">Isometric shoulder elevation strength for the 3 scapular positions.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Isometric strength was significantly lower for the SP position compared with the SN position (8.5 +/- 3.4 kg vs 11.1 +/- 4.0 kg, P &lt;.0005) and for the SR position relative to the SN position (7.8 +/- 3.3 kg vs 11.1 +/- 4.0 kg, P &lt;.00003). Strength values did not differ between the SP and SR positions (P =.38).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Movement of the scapula into a protracted or retracted position results in a statistically significant reduction in isometric shoulder elevation strength as measured in this study. Further research is warranted to examine the relationship between scapular position and shoulder muscle function.</AbstractText>
